# HomeCore @VERSION@ (linux/@GOARCH@)

Release package of HomeCore: one static binary (`homecore`, CGO off, no runtime dependencies) that
runs as **Controller** (API + web UI + SQLite), **Agent** (signed shutdown on a managed machine) or both.

This file is written for an installing human **or AI agent**. Every step is a shell command; follow them
in order and run the **Verify** command of each section before moving on.

| | |
|---|---|
| Version | `@VERSION@` |
| Commit | `@COMMIT@` |
| Target | linux / `@GOARCH@` (`@UNAME_M@` in `uname -m`) |
| Database schema | version `@SCHEMA_VERSION@` (newest migration in this package) |
| Web UI | embedded in the binary, nothing to install separately |
| Needs | Linux with systemd; `root` (sudo) for install. Agent power actions need polkit |

Check this package matches the machine before installing: `uname -m` must print `@UNAME_M@`.

## Package contents

```
homecore                              the binary (Controller, Agent and CLI in one)
VERSION                               version, commit, target, schema version
README.md                             this file
configs/homecore.example.yaml         all config keys with defaults and comments
deploy/systemd/homecore-controller.service
deploy/systemd/homecore-agent.service
deploy/polkit/50-homecore-agent.rules  lets only the agent user power off / reboot (Agent only)
migrations/*.sql                      database migrations, FOR REFERENCE ONLY
```

**Migrations are compiled into the binary and applied automatically** when the Controller starts
(each in its own transaction, oldest first, already-applied ones skipped). You never run the files in
`migrations/` by hand; they are shipped so you can read what an upgrade changes in the schema.
The Agent has no database.

## Which role to install

| Machine | Install |
|---|---|
| The one machine that hosts the API, the web UI and the database | **Controller** |
| Every machine the Controller must be able to shut down | **Agent** (separate machine from the Controller, or the same one) |

A Controller needs no Agent to work (device CRUD, TCP monitoring, Wake-on-LAN, DDNS). Install Agents only
for remote shutdown.

Run all commands below from the directory where this archive was extracted (`pwd` contains `homecore`).

---

## Install: Controller

```bash
set -euo pipefail

sudo useradd --system --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin homecore || true   # ok if it exists
sudo install -m 0755 homecore /usr/local/bin/homecore
sudo install -d -m 0750 -o root -g homecore /etc/homecore
# Do not overwrite an existing config on re-run:
[ -f /etc/homecore/homecore.yaml ] || sudo install -m 0640 -g homecore configs/homecore.example.yaml /etc/homecore/homecore.yaml
sudo install -m 0644 deploy/systemd/homecore-controller.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
```

Edit `/etc/homecore/homecore.yaml` if needed (listen address, TLS, `trusted_proxies`, DDNS). The defaults
work out of the box: listen `:8080`, data in `/var/lib/homecore` (created by systemd `StateDirectory`).
The SQLite database is `/var/lib/homecore/homecore.db` (see [Data and secrets](#data-and-secrets) to move it).

```bash
sudo systemctl enable --now homecore-controller
```

**Verify**

```bash
systemctl is-active homecore-controller                 # active
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz                 # HTTP 200
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8080/readyz                  # HTTP 200 once the schema is current
homecore --version                                      # @VERSION@ (@COMMIT@)
```

### First account (required: there is no default account)

```bash
# Run as the service user so the database file keeps its owner. Prompts for the password twice (min 12 chars).
sudo -u homecore homecore user add --config /etc/homecore/homecore.yaml <username>
# Non-interactive (script): HOMECORE_PASSWORD=<secret> sudo -E -u homecore homecore user add --config /etc/homecore/homecore.yaml <username>
```

Then open `http://<controller-host>:8080/` and sign in. API docs: `http://<controller-host>:8080/api/docs`.

### Controller public key (needed by Agents)

```bash
sudo -u homecore homecore key show --config /etc/homecore/homecore.yaml
```

Copy the printed base64 public key; each Agent trusts it in `agent.trusted_controllers`.

---

## Install: Agent (on each managed machine)

### Option A (recommended): the command from the web UI, no config file

The Agent takes its id and the Controller public key as arguments, so it needs no config file and stores
nothing on the machine.

1. In the web UI open the device, **Edit > Remote Shutdown**, enable the integration (a new Agent ID is
   generated, or press **Generate**), pick the **Linux** tab and copy the command. Save the device.
2. On the managed machine, in the folder where this package was extracted, run the binary with nothing
   but the mode, in a terminal, and paste the command when it asks:

```bash
./homecore --mode agent
```

   It writes `homecore-agent.service` and `50-homecore-agent.rules` in the current folder, prints the
   exact `sudo` lines that install them (user, binary, polkit rule, `systemctl enable --now`) and the
   check commands, then exits. It installs nothing itself. Run the printed lines.

Running the pasted command directly (`./homecore --mode agent --agent-id ... --trusted-controller ...`)
starts the Agent in the foreground instead: handy to try it before installing the service.

**Verify**

```bash
systemctl is-active homecore-agent                   # active
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9750/agent/v1/healthz       # {"agent_id":"<the id shown in the UI>","status":"ok",...}
```

The web UI shows the Agent as *Ready* within a minute. Without a terminal (scripts, AI agents) the prompt
cannot run: use Option B, or write the unit yourself with the command from the UI as `ExecStart`.

### Option B: config file

Needs the Controller public key from above.

```bash
set -euo pipefail

sudo useradd --system --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin homecore-agent || true
sudo install -m 0755 homecore /usr/local/bin/homecore
sudo install -d -m 0750 -o root -g homecore-agent /etc/homecore
sudo install -m 0644 deploy/polkit/50-homecore-agent.rules /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/
sudo install -m 0644 deploy/systemd/homecore-agent.service /etc/systemd/system/
```

Create `/etc/homecore/agent.yaml` (mode 0640, group `homecore-agent`) **if it does not exist**:

```yaml
mode: agent
agent:
  listen: ":9750"            # LAN only. Never port-forward; use a VPN for remote access.
  allowed_actions: [shutdown]   # shutdown | reboot
  trusted_controllers:
    - name: my-controller
      public_key: "<base64 public key from `homecore key show`>"
```

```bash
sudo chown root:homecore-agent /etc/homecore/agent.yaml && sudo chmod 0640 /etc/homecore/agent.yaml
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now homecore-agent
```

**Verify**

```bash
systemctl is-active homecore-agent                       # active
journalctl -u homecore-agent --no-pager | grep agent_id  # the id to bind to the device on the Controller
```

Then, in the web UI, bind that `agent_id` (and the agent URL, `http://<agent-ip>:9750`) to the device.

Notes
- polkit older than 0.106 (old Debian/Ubuntu) needs an equivalent `.pkla` file instead of the `.rules` JS file.
- Keep the Agent port closed to the Internet (firewall it to the LAN / VPN subnet).
- Option B only: `agent.id` in the config file (or `HOMECORE_AGENT_ID`) makes the Agent use an id from the UI instead of creating one in `/var/lib/homecore-agent/agent_id`.
- Agent and Controller can be installed on the same machine; use the two systemd units, they use separate users and data dirs.

