## Upgrade

Upgrades replace the binary and the unit files; **config and data are never touched** by the steps below.
The Controller applies new migrations itself on the first start of the new binary.

**Order: upgrade the Controller first, then the Agents.** Agents of an older release keep working with a
newer Controller unless the release notes of the new version say the protocol changed.

### Upgrade the Controller

```bash
set -euo pipefail

# 1. Record where we are.
homecore --version
sudo sqlite3 /var/lib/homecore/homecore.db 'SELECT MAX(version) FROM schema_migrations;' 2>/dev/null || true  # optional, sqlite3 CLI may be absent

# 2. Stop, then back up the WHOLE data directory (database, master.key, WAL files). Rollback needs this.
sudo systemctl stop homecore-controller
sudo cp -a /var/lib/homecore "/var/lib/homecore.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"
sudo cp -a /usr/local/bin/homecore /usr/local/bin/homecore.prev

# 3. Install the new binary and unit file (extracted from this package), keep the existing config.
sudo install -m 0755 homecore /usr/local/bin/homecore
sudo install -m 0644 deploy/systemd/homecore-controller.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# 4. Start. New migrations run now.
sudo systemctl start homecore-controller
```

**Verify** (all must pass; otherwise roll back)

```bash
systemctl is-active homecore-controller                 # active
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8080/readyz                  # HTTP 200 (503 = schema behind the binary)
homecore --version                                      # @VERSION@ (@COMMIT@)
journalctl -u homecore-controller -n 30 --no-pager      # "database ready ... migrations_applied=N schema_version=@SCHEMA_VERSION@"
```

Compare `/etc/homecore/homecore.yaml` with `configs/homecore.example.yaml` of this package: new keys have
defaults, but **unknown keys are rejected at startup** (a key removed in a newer release will stop the
service; the journal names it).

### Upgrade an Agent

```bash
set -euo pipefail
sudo systemctl stop homecore-agent
sudo cp -a /usr/local/bin/homecore /usr/local/bin/homecore.prev
sudo install -m 0755 homecore /usr/local/bin/homecore
sudo install -m 0644 deploy/systemd/homecore-agent.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo install -m 0644 deploy/polkit/50-homecore-agent.rules /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start homecore-agent
systemctl is-active homecore-agent
homecore --version
```

The Agent keeps its `agent_id` (stored in its data dir), so the device binding survives upgrades.

### Roll back

**There is no schema downgrade, and an older binary does not refuse a newer database.** After a migration
has run, putting the old binary back is not enough: restore the data backup taken before the upgrade.

```bash
sudo systemctl stop homecore-controller
sudo install -m 0755 /usr/local/bin/homecore.prev /usr/local/bin/homecore
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/homecore
sudo mv /var/lib/homecore.bak-<timestamp> /var/lib/homecore
sudo systemctl start homecore-controller
```

If the upgrade did not start (no migration ran, `migrations_applied=0`), restoring the binary alone is enough.

