## Data and secrets

| Path | Holds | Back up? |
|---|---|---|
| `/etc/homecore/homecore.yaml`, `/etc/homecore/agent.yaml` | configuration | yes |
| `/var/lib/homecore/homecore.db` (+ `-wal`, `-shm`) | SQLite database | yes (stop the service, or copy all three files together) |
| `/var/lib/homecore/master.key` | key that seals every secret in the database | **yes, and keep it with the database**: without it the sealed secrets (Cloudflare tokens, signing keys) cannot be read and the Controller will not replace them |
| `/var/lib/homecore-agent/` | the Agent identity (`agent_id`); only exists for an Agent installed with Option B (Option A keeps the id in the unit file) | yes |

Never put secrets in logs, tickets or chat. The files above are `0600`/`0640` on purpose.

### Where the database lives and how to move it

The Controller database is always `<data_dir>/homecore.db`, and `master.key` sits next to it. `data_dir` is
resolved in this order (first match wins):

1. `--data-dir DIR` (only the `user`, `token` and `key` subcommands)
2. environment variable `HOMECORE_DATA_DIR`
3. `data_dir:` in the config file
4. default `./data`, relative to the working directory of the process

**The packaged unit sets `HOMECORE_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/homecore`, so changing `data_dir:` in
`/etc/homecore/homecore.yaml` has no effect for the service.** To move the data, use a drop-in:

```bash
sudo systemctl stop homecore-controller
sudo mkdir -p /srv/homecore && sudo chown homecore:homecore /srv/homecore && sudo chmod 0700 /srv/homecore
sudo cp -a /var/lib/homecore/. /srv/homecore/          # homecore.db, -wal, -shm and master.key together
sudo systemctl edit homecore-controller                # add the three lines below, save
#   [Service]
#   Environment=HOMECORE_DATA_DIR=/srv/homecore
#   ReadWritePaths=/srv/homecore
sudo systemctl start homecore-controller
```

`ReadWritePaths` is required: the unit uses `ProtectSystem=strict`, so the service can only write to its
`StateDirectory` (`/var/lib/homecore`) unless another path is listed. The `homecore user|token|key`
commands you run by hand must use the same directory (`HOMECORE_DATA_DIR=/srv/homecore` or `--data-dir`).
Verify: `sudo ls -l /srv/homecore/homecore.db` and `journalctl -u homecore-controller -n 20` shows the new `data_dir`.

## Port forwarding below port 1024 (optional)

The Controller can forward ports (web UI: **Port Forwarding**). The shipped unit drops every capability, so a rule on a
port below 1024 (80, 443, 22 ...) shows `permission denied`; ports from 1024 up work as they are. To allow low ports:

```bash
sudo systemctl edit homecore-controller
# [Service]
# AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
# CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
sudo systemctl restart homecore-controller
```

A rule on `0.0.0.0` is reachable from every network of the host: restrict it with the host firewall or choose a
specific interface in the rule. Enabled rules start again after a restart and are retried every 15 s while they cannot
bind.

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Check |
|---|---|
| Service fails at start with an `unknown field` / config error | `journalctl -u homecore-controller -n 50`; remove or rename the key it names, compare with `configs/homecore.example.yaml` |
| `/readyz` returns 503 | Database schema is behind the binary; look for a failed `migration NNNN_...` line in the journal |
| `exec format error` | Wrong architecture package; `uname -m` must print `@UNAME_M@` |
| Agent never shows up / shutdown rejected | The Controller public key in `agent.trusted_controllers` is wrong or missing; re-run `homecore key show` on the Controller |
| Cannot sign in | No account yet: run `homecore user add` (above); reset with `homecore user passwd` |

## Uninstall

```bash
sudo systemctl disable --now homecore-controller homecore-agent 2>/dev/null || true
sudo rm -f /etc/systemd/system/homecore-{controller,agent}.service /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-homecore-agent.rules /usr/local/bin/homecore{,.prev}
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
# Data and config are kept on purpose. To delete them too: /var/lib/homecore*, /etc/homecore
```
